        "advisability": "inadvisable",
        "reason": "Carrier A (url): QUERY that returns a signed URL — it has a side effect (generates signature with expiry), but conceptually it's a query operation. Carrier B (upload): COMMAND that creates/changes file state in storage. Carrier C (download): COMMAND that retrieves file to local path. These violate CQS in different directions: url() is a query with hidden side effect (signature generation), while upload/download are pure commands. Even more critically, url() might be called for a file that doesn't exist yet (to pre-generate URL before upload), but exists() is a separate query. Unifying these would create a method that is simultaneously query-and-command — a VIOLATION of CQS."
